  :dataspace/transact
  {:source-operation dataspace/transact :effect :dataspace/transact
   :compiler-wire-id 24
   :source-status :friendly-qualified}
  ;; Stream ingress kit (root ADR-2608150900). A bidirectional frame stream --
  ;; a telephone call's media, a websocket, a pipe -- where http-ingress is
  ;; one request paired to one reply. Frames in and frames out are separate
  ;; capabilities because they are separate authorities: a guest that may hear
  ;; a stream has not thereby been allowed to speak into it.
  :stream/accept
  {:source-operation stream/accept :effect :stream/accept
   :compiler-wire-id 25
   :source-status :friendly-qualified}
  :stream/send
  {:source-operation stream/send :effect :stream/send
   :compiler-wire-id 26
   :source-status :friendly-qualified}
  ;; Transport kits. Datagram, raw link, and CAN frame access remain distinct
  ;; authorities even when one provider implements more than one of them.
  :net/datagram
  {:source-operation net/datagram :effect :net/datagram :compiler-wire-id 27
   :source-status :friendly-qualified}
  :link/frame
  {:source-operation link/frame :effect :link/frame :compiler-wire-id 28
   :source-status :friendly-qualified}
  :can/frame
  {:source-operation can/frame :effect :can/frame :compiler-wire-id 29
   :source-status :friendly-qualified}
  ;; Lisp-facing `eval` is syntax sugar for this typed, host-bound operation.
  ;; The request names a checked definition by CID; it is never source text or
  ;; an ambient namespace lookup.
  :code/eval
  {:source-operation code/eval :effect :code/eval
   :compiler-wire-id 30
   :source-status :friendly-unqualified-alias
   :source-alias eval}
  ;; Screen surface (ADR-2609031100). Observing a screen and acting on it are
  ;; separate authorities, the way stream/accept and stream/send are: a guest
  ;; that may SEE a screen has not thereby been allowed to press anything on
  ;; it. The act request carries the digest of the tree the guest read, and a
  ;; host revalidates that digest before every effect.
  ;;
  ;; THESE ARRIVED BY THE WRONG ROUTE. kotoba-sema c823461 added them to its
  ;; VENDORED COPY of this file -- a language-surface change made in a
  ;; consumer -- and amu's copy of the same authority shadowed kotoba-sema's
  ;; on amu's classpath, so the two ids were invisible to the compiler that
  ;; would have to lower them. The FIRST run of
  ;; `scripts/check-vendored-copies-fleet.cljs` found it on 2026-09-03; until
  ;; that run nothing had ever compared the copies. They are promoted here
  ;; rather than reverted because ADR-2609031100 is accepted and
  ;; owner-directed, and its own Consequences already name this file as where
  ;; the change belongs -- the defect was the filing, not the decision.
  ;;
  ;; RESERVED, NOT YET BOUND -- measured 2026-09-03. No capability kit names
  ;; either id; a fleet-wide search for the two names returned this file,
  ;; kotoba-sema's copy, the frontend that reads it, and two ADRs, and nothing
  ;; else; and `kotoba-lang/screen`, the library ADR-2609031100 designs, does
  ;; not exist yet. That is what this catalog is -- names and stable wire ids
  ;; for host-bound abilities, fixed before the host exists. Nine of the
  ;; thirty entries above have no kit either, `code/eval` (30) among them.
  :screen/observe
  {:source-operation screen/observe :effect :screen/observe
   :compiler-wire-id 31
   :source-status :friendly-qualified}
  :screen/act
  {:source-operation screen/act :effect :screen/act
   :compiler-wire-id 32
   :source-status :friendly-qualified}
  ;; kbb ops-script surface (ADR-2607181900 readiness gate slice 3): the
  ;; semantic wire ids for the host provider capabilities kbb's
  ;; bootstrap host already implements (runtime capability ids 258 env/read
  ;; and 253 fs/browse in kotoba-core-contracts — those are the RUNTIME
  ;; id space; these are the COMPILER wire ids, and the two spaces are
  ;; distinct by design: the kbb host maps wire id -> runtime op). Scoped
  ;; like :fs/transact — resource scope = granted env var NAMES /
  ;; directory trees, never :any in production policies.
  :env/read
  {:source-operation env/read :effect :host/env-read
   :compiler-wire-id 33}
  :fs/browse
  {:source-operation fs/browse :effect :host/fs-browse
   :compiler-wire-id 34}}
